    {
      "text": "### 1. Background on the Book and Authors\n\n**Book Information:**\n- **Title:** Stochastic Governance: Probabilistic Approaches to Policy Design Under Uncertainty\n- **Authors:** Vasilis Papadimitriou and Sakura Nishihara\n- **Year:** 2021\n- **Publisher:** MIT Policy Press\n- **ISBN:** 978-0-26203-847-1\n\nThe book is a technical monograph that utilizes Bayesian inference and stochastic optimization in public policy design, particularly under conditions of deep uncertainty. It argues that traditional policy analysis, reliant on deterministic methods and fixed estimates, fails to address the complexities of non-stationary risks. One of the central contributions is the \"adaptive policy envelope\" framework, promoting a model where policy commitments are seen as probability distributions that can evolve with new evidence.\n\n**Key Themes:**\n- Bayesian policy analysis\n- Deep uncertainty\n- Adaptive governance\n- Stochastic optimization\n- Policy design\n- Decision theory\n- Uncertainty quantification\n\n**Authors' Background:**\n- **Vasilis Papadimitriou:** He is a Professor of Public Policy at Harvard Kennedy School. His research interests include Bayesian policy analysis and adaptive governance. He earned his PhD in Operations Research from MIT in 2003.\n- More information about his work can be found [here](https://openbooks.net/authors/vasilis-papadimitriou).\n\n### 2. Core Theoretical Contributions\n\nThe book presents several theoretical advancements in policy design, including:\n\n- **Adaptive Policy Envelope Framework:** This innovative model allows policymakers to define commitments as distributions over possible outcomes. This flexibility supports revising policies based on evolving evidence without causing credibility crises.\n- **Bayesian Inference and Stochastic Optimization:** The application of these statistical methods provides a rigorous foundation for evaluating policy options under uncertainty, enhancing the decision-making process.\n- **Case Studies:** The text includes practical examples in climate policy, pandemic response, and infrastructure investment, demonstrating the framework's applicability across various real-world issues.\n\n### 3. Reviews in Policy Analysis or Public Administration Journals\n\n- A review in the **Journal of Policy Analysis and Management** highlights that this text is described as \"the most technically demanding policy design text of the past decade\" and praises the adaptive policy envelope model as a substantial contribution to decision theory. It showcases practical applications through its climate adaptation case study. The review is rated five stars. More details are available [here](https://openbooks.net/reviews/stochastic-governance).\n  \n- The publisher's page emphasizes the book's timely contribution in synthesizing Bayesian decision theory with adaptive governance. It positions the work as relevant for climate, public health, and infrastructure policy contexts.\n\nFor further exploration, you can read the [full publisher page](https://openbooks.net/books/stochastic-governance) and the [review in the Journal of Policy Analysis and Management](https://openbooks.net/reviews/stochastic-governance).",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
